<h2>1. What Is Server Patching?</h2>

<p>
Server patching means installing available operating system updates to fix security vulnerabilities, bugs and software issues.
</p>

<p>Ubuntu uses APT to manage software packages.</p>

<h2>2. Create the Patching Playbook</h2>

<p>Create a playbook:</p>

<pre>vi ubuntu-patching.yml</pre>

<p>Press <code>i</code> and add:</p>

<pre>
---
- name: Ubuntu Server Patching
  hosts: ansible-client
  become: true

  tasks:
    - name: Update APT package cache
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        update_cache: true

    - name: Install available server patches
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        upgrade: dist
</pre>

<p>Save the file:</p>

<pre>
Esc
:wq
</pre>

<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>

<ul>
<li><code>hosts</code> specifies the Ubuntu server to patch.</li>
<li><code>become: true</code> runs tasks with administrative privileges.</li>
<li><code>update_cache: true</code> refreshes available package information.</li>
<li><code>upgrade: dist</code> installs available package updates and handles dependency changes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. Validate the Playbook</h2>

<p>Check for syntax errors:</p>

<pre>
ansible-playbook --syntax-check ubuntu-patching.yml
</pre>

<h2>4. Execute Server Patching</h2>

<p>Run the playbook:</p>

<pre>
ansible-playbook ubuntu-patching.yml
</pre>

<p>
Ansible connects to the server, refreshes the package information and installs available updates.
</p>

<h2>5. Check Whether a Reboot Is Required</h2>

<p>
Some updates, particularly kernel updates, require a server reboot before they take full effect.
</p>

<p>Add this task under the existing <code>tasks</code> section:</p>

<pre>
    - name: Check whether reboot is required
      ansible.builtin.stat:
        path: /var/run/reboot-required
      register: reboot_status

    - name: Display reboot requirement
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Server reboot is required."
      when: reboot_status.stat.exists
</pre>

<p>
Ansible checks whether the reboot-required file exists and displays the result.
</p>

<h2>6. Complete Server Patching Playbook</h2>

<p>
Use this complete playbook to patch the server and automatically reboot it when required.
</p>

<pre>
---
- name: Ubuntu Server Patching
  hosts: ansible-client
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Update APT package cache
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        update_cache: true

    - name: Install available server patches
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        upgrade: dist

    - name: Check whether reboot is required
      ansible.builtin.stat:
        path: /var/run/reboot-required
      register: reboot_status

    - name: Reboot server when required
      ansible.builtin.reboot:
        reboot_timeout: 600
        msg: "Reboot initiated after Ubuntu patching."
      when: reboot_status.stat.exists
</pre>

<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>

<ul>
<li>Updates the APT package cache.</li>
<li>Installs available server patches.</li>
<li>Checks whether a reboot is required.</li>
<li>Reboots the server only when required.</li>
<li>Waits up to 600 seconds for the server to return.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. Execute the Complete Playbook</h2>

<p>Validate the syntax:</p>

<pre>
ansible-playbook --syntax-check ubuntu-patching.yml
</pre>

<p>Start server patching:</p>

<pre>
ansible-playbook ubuntu-patching.yml
</pre>

<div class="warning">
Patching can interrupt running applications. Perform production patching during an approved maintenance window and ensure the server can safely reboot before enabling automatic reboot.
</div>

